        "number": 89,
        "repo": "sonic-fips",
        "comment_at": "2026-08-07T23:32:31Z",
        "comment_author": "saiarcot895",
        "comment_body": "> the shell spec exempts `if`-body commands from `-e`\r\n\r\nThis is incorrect. `if`-body commands are not excluded from `-e`, except in very specific circumstances. Quoting from [the manual](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/The-Set-Builtin.html):\r\n\r\n> The shell does not exit if the command that fails is part of the command list immediately following a while or until reserved word, part of the test in an if statement, part of any command executed in a && or || list except the command following the final && or ||, any command in a pipeline but the last (subject to the state of the pipefail shell option), or if the command\u2019s return status is being inverted with !. If a compound command other than a subshell returns a non-zero status because a command failed while -e was being ignored, the shell does not exit.\r\n\r\nIn this case, because the `quilt push -a` is in the middle of `&&` commands, and not the last one, this is the reason why the shell does not exit, and not because it's in an `if` block.\r\n\r\nThe simplest fix is the following:\r\n\r\n```diff\r\ndiff --git a/Makefile b/Makefile\r\nindex 38d11f3..647a79f 100644\r\n--- a/Makefile\r\n+++ b/Makefile\r\n@@ -40,8 +40,8 @@ $(addprefix $(TARGET_PATH)/, $(MAIN_TARGETS)) : $(TARGET_PATH)/% : $$(addprefix\r\n        # Copy debian folder\r\n        if [ -n \"$($*_DEBIAN)\" ]; then mkdir -p $($*_SRC_PATH)/debian; cp $($*_DEBIAN)/* -rf $($*_SRC_PATH)/debian/; fi\r\n        # Apply series of patches if exist\r\n-       if [ -f $($*_SRC_PATH).patch/series ]; then pushd $($*_SRC_PATH) && QUILT_PATCHES=../$(notdir $($*_SRC_PATH)).patch quilt push -a && mv .pc .pc1; popd; fi\r\n-       if [ -n \"$($*_PATCH_EXT)\" ]; then pushd $($*_SRC_PATH); QUILT_PATCHES=$($*_PATCH_EXT) quilt push -a && mv .pc .pc2; popd; fi\r\n+       if [ -f $($*_SRC_PATH).patch/series ]; then pushd $($*_SRC_PATH) && QUILT_PATCHES=../$(notdir $($*_SRC_PATH)).patch quilt push -a; mv .pc .pc1; popd; fi\r\n+       if [ -n \"$($*_PATCH_EXT)\" ]; then pushd $($*_SRC_PATH); QUILT_PATCHES=$($*_PATCH_EXT) quilt push -a; mv .pc .pc2; popd; fi\r\n        # Merge the debian patches if not applied\r\n        if [ -f $($*_SRC_PATH).patch/debian.patch/series ]; then\r\n          LAST_PATCH=$$(tail -n1  $($*_SRC_PATH).patch/debian.patch/series)\r\n```"
    },
    {
        "number": 89,
        "repo": "sonic-fips",
        "comment_at": "2026-08-08T00:37:38Z",
        "comment_author": "xq9mend",
        "comment_body": "You're right \u2014 thank you for the correction. The real reason `quilt push -a` failure did not abort the build was that it was the non-final command in a `&&` chain (per the `-e` rule you quoted), not because it was in an `if` body.\n\nThe current implementation uses `set +e` / `QUILT_RC` explicitly rather than relying on shell exit behavior, which handles both the failure case and rc=2 (\"all patches already applied\"). The PR description was misleading though \u2014 I've noted the correct explanation for the record."
    },
